# hc-patient-app — what Prettier does not format
#
# One job: keep generated and vendored files out of scope. It had a second one until backlog item 23
# (2026-09-09) — the deliberate exclusion of `*.html` — and that block is gone because the reason
# under it was wrong. See `.prettierrc`, which now formats templates with the `angular` parser, and
# the note at the bottom of this file.
#
# This file exists so that a bare `npx prettier --check .` agrees with `npm run prettier:check`.
# Before it, the two disagreed: the npm scripts glob narrowly enough to miss `android/**/build/**`,
# thousands of generated Gradle files that the bare form walks, so they passed by luck rather than
# by design.
#
# An earlier draft of this paragraph also blamed the merged i18n bundles. That was wrong and is
# worth correcting rather than deleting: `src/assets/i18n/*.json` and `src/environments/i18n-hash.ts`
# were always inside the npm glob. They pass because `JSON.stringify(sorted, null, 2)` is already
# Prettier's output shape, not because anything missed them. They are listed below as generated
# output, which is a different and better reason.

# --- NOT a rule any more: `*.html` was here, and the reason under it was wrong ------------------
#
# `*.html` was excluded from Prettier by backlog item 16 (2026-09-08) under the claim that "NO
# PRETTIER VERSION INDENTS INSIDE ANGULAR CONTROL FLOW", measured against every release from 2.8.8
# to 4.0.0-alpha.13. The sweep was real; the conclusion drawn from it was not. Flattening is a
# PARSER-INFERENCE property, not a version one: `*.component.html` infers Prettier's `angular`
# parser, which indents `@if`/`@for` correctly, and every other name infers the generic `html`
# parser, which flattens. This repo's templates are Ionic `*.page.html`, so they took the flattening
# parser at every version — which is exactly what the sweep measured, and exactly what it did not
# explain. Backlog item 23 (2026-09-09) is the correction; `.prettierrc` now carries a one-line
# `overrides` block naming the `angular` parser, and the templates are formatted again.
#
# This paragraph stays because the exclusion was placed here deliberately, at the point somebody
# would come to undo it, and it argued hard against being undone. Anybody arriving with that memory
# should find out here that it went, and why, rather than concluding the glob was widened carelessly.
#
# The measurement that mattered, before it was widened: 29 templates, 13 disagreeing with the pin
# under the inferred parser and 19 under `angular` — more files moving, but in the direction item 16
# wanted. `profile.page.html`, the 210-line template item 16 existed to protect, keeps its
# hand-indentation ENTIRELY under `angular` (22 changed lines, not one of them an `@if`/`@for`),
# while `tabs.page.html` and `login.page.html` are re-indented OUT of the flattened state the
# exclusion had been preserving them in. Measured on cfe4012 with prettier 3.1.0.
#
# Template CORRECTNESS is a separate gate and always was: `.eslintrc.json` has an `*.html` override
# running @angular-eslint/eslint-plugin-template, `npm run lint` is `eslint . --ext
# .js,.mjs,.ts,.html`, and CI runs it. Its two blind spots are recorded in `.eslintrc.json`, next to
# the rules they qualify, rather than here.

